  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is \n\n$$\n{C}_{1}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation defines the single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane. \n\nNotice that the other equations do not define a single point: \n\n- ${C}_{2}\\,:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting lines in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- ${C}_{3}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{4}=1$ is the equation of a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $2.$\n\n- ${C}_{4}\\,:\\,{x}_{2}=2{x}_{1}^{2}$ is the equation of a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive ${x}_{2}$-axis.\n\n- ${C}_{5}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{9}=-1$ has no points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane that satisfy the equation (imaginary ellipse).",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
